"[2602.14490v1] Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of LLMs with Mixture of Space Experts (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.14490v1)\nciteturn31894view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn31893view0\",\"lineno\":15}); Total lines: 168\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Machine Learning\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 16 Feb 2026]\nL9: # Title:Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of LLMs with Mixture of Space Experts\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Buze Zhang , cite6†Jinkai Tao , cite7†Zilang Zeng , cite8†Neil He , cite9†Ali Maatouk , cite10†Menglin Yang , cite11†Rex Ying L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of LLMs with Mixture of Space Experts, by Buze Zhang and 6 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large Language Models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable progress, with Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) emerging as a key technique for downstream task adaptation. However, existing PEFT methods mainly operate in Euclidean space, fundamentally limiting their capacity to capture complex geometric structures inherent in language data.\nL17: While alternative geometric spaces, like hyperbolic geometries for hierarchical data and spherical manifolds for circular patterns, offer theoretical advantages, forcing representations into a single manifold type ultimately limits expressiveness, even when curvature parameters are learnable. To address this, we propose Mixture of Space (MoS), a unified framework that leverages multiple geometric spaces simultaneously to learn richer, curvature-aware representations.\nL18: Building on this scheme, we develop MoSLoRA, which extends Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) with heterogeneous geometric experts, enabling models to dynamically select or combine appropriate geometric spaces based on input context. Furthermore, to address the computational overhead of frequent manifold switching, we develop a lightweight routing mechanism. Moreover, we provide empirical insights into how curvature optimization impacts training stability and model performance.\nL19: Our experiments across diverse benchmarks demonstrate that MoSLoRA consistently outperforms strong baselines, achieving up to 5.6% improvement on MATH500 and 15.9% on MAWPS.\nL20: Comments:  | 15 pages, 11 figures\nL21: Subjects:  | Machine Learning (cs.LG); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite14†arXiv:2602.14490 [cs.LG]\nL23:    | (or cite15†arXiv:2602.14490v1 [cs.LG] for this version)\nL24:    | cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.14490†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Buze Zhang [cite17†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 16 Feb 2026 06:07:32 UTC (873 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning of LLMs with Mixture of Space Experts, by Buze Zhang and 6 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite12†View PDF L37:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite18†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite19†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.LG\nL44: \nL45: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L46: \nL47: cite22†new | cite23†recent | cite24†2026-02 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite25†cs L52: cite26†cs.AI L53: cite27†cs.CL L54: cite28†cs.NE L55: \nL56: ### References & Citations\nL57: \nL58:   * cite29†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L59:   * cite30†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L60:   * cite31†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L61: \n