ork with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMAS-on-the-Fly: Dynamic Adaptation of LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems at Test Time (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13671v1)\nciteturn31894view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13671v1\",\"lineno\":60}); Total lines: 494\nL0: ##### Report GitHub Issue\nL1: \nL2: [Button: ×]\nL3: \nL4: Title: [Input: Enter title]\nL5: \nL6: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below:\nL7: \nL8: Description:\nL9: \nL10: [Button: Submit without GitHub] [Button: Submit in GitHub]\nL11: \nL12: cite0†Back to arXiv L13: \nL14: cite1†Why HTML?†info.arxiv.org cite2†Report Issue cite3†Back to Abstract cite4†Download PDF L15:   1. cite5†Abstract L16:   2. cite6†1 Introduction L17:   3. cite7†2 Related Work L18:     1. cite8†2.1 LLM-based Multi-Agent System L19:     2. cite9†2.2 Learning at Test Time for LLMs L20:     3. cite10†2.3 Memory in Multi-Agent System L21:   4. cite11†3 Proposed Method L22:     1. cite12†3.1 Overall Framework L23:     2. cite13†3.2 SOP-Guided System Generation L24:     3. cite14†3.3 Process Supervised Execution L25:     4. cite15†3.4 Reflective Experience Distillation L26:     5. cite16†3.5 Comparison with Existing Works L27:   5. cite17†4 Experiment L28:     1. cite18†4.1 Experimental Setup L29:       1. cite19†Benchmarks. L30:       2. cite20†Baselines. L31:       3. cite21†Implementation details. L32:     2. cite22†4.2 Performance Comparison L33:     3. cite23†4.3 Robustness Analysis L34:     4. cite24†4.4 Ablation Study L35:       1. cite25†Effect of SOP Guidance. L36:       2. cite26†Effect of Process Supervision. L37:     5. cite27†4.5 Case Study L38:   6. cite28†5 Conclusion L39:   7. cite29†References L40:   8. cite30†A Comparison with Related Works L41:   9. cite31†B Implementation Details L42:     1. cite32†B.1 Details of the Repository L43:       1. cite33†B.1.1 Repository Initialization L44:       2. cite34†B.1.2 Repository Update L45:     2. cite35†B.2 Agent Communication Details L46:     3. cite36†B.3 Experimental Setup L47:       1. cite37†B.3.1 Datasets and Evaluation Metrics L48:       2. cite38†B.3.2 Baselines L49:       3. cite39†B.3.3 Hyperparameters L50:   10. cite40†C Complementary Experiments L51:     1. cite41†C.1 Impact of Watcher Intervention Frequency L52:     2. cite42†C.2 Effect of Hybrid Retriever L53:     3. cite43†C.3 Generalization across Different Backbone L54:     4. cite44†C.4 Supplementary Efficiency Comparison L55:   11. cite45†D Case Study L56: cite46†License: CC BY-NC-ND 4.0†info.arxiv.org L57: \nL58: arXiv:2602.13671v1 [cs.MA] 14 Feb 2026\nL59: # MAS-on-the-Fly: Dynamic Adaptation of LLM-based\nL60: Multi-Agent Systems at Test Time\nL61: \nL62: Guangyi Liu Affiliation: Department of Electronic Engineering, Tsinghua University Email: liugy24@mails.tsinghua.edu.cn    Haojun Lin Affiliation: Ant Group Email: qyaoaa@tsinghua.edu.cn    Huan Zeng Affiliation: Ant Group    Heng Wang Affiliation: Ant Group    Quanming Yao ^{†}^{†}thanks: Correspondence is to Q.Yao. Affiliation: Department of Electronic Engineering, Tsinghua University\nL63: ###### Abstract\nL64: Large Language Model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems (MAS) have emerged as a promising paradigm for solving complex tasks. However, existing works often rely on manual designs or “one-size-fits-all” automation, lacking dynamic adaptability after deployment. Inspired by how biological systems adapt, we introduce MASFly, a novel multi-agent framework enabling dynamic adaptation at test time.\nL65: To adapt system generation, MASFly employs a retrieval-augmented SOP instantiation mechanism that leverages a self-constructed repository of successful collaboration patterns, enabling the LLM to assemble customized MASs for new queries. For adaptive execution, MASFly incorporates an experience-guided supervision mechanism, where a dedicated Watcher agent monitors system behaviors with reference to a personalized experience pool and provides real-time interventions.\nL66: Extensive experiments demonstrate that MASFly achieves state-of-the-art performance, most notably a 61.7% success rate on the TravelPlanner benchmark, while exhibiting strong task adaptability and robustness.\nL67: ## 1 Introduction\nL68: The remarkable advancements in Large Language Models (LLMs) have transformed the field of artificial intelligence, showing remarkable abilities in reasoning and generation cite47†Achiam et al. (2023) ; cite48†Liu et al. (2024) ; cite49†Yang et al. (2025) . However, tackling complex, multi-faceted problems often exceeds the capacity of a single LLM cite50†Talebirad and Nadiri (2023) . This has led to the rapid emergence of LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) cite51†Hong et al. (2023) ; cite52†Wu et al.\nL69: (2024) , where multiple agents take on specialized roles and work collaboratively, demonstrating human-like collective intelligence for advanced problem-solving.\nL70: cite53†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1: Drawing inspiration from biological adaptation, MASFly achieves dynamic adaptation at test-time, enabling adaptive system generation via SOP instantiation and adaptive execution via process supervision.\nL71: Early LLM-based MASs, like MetaGPT cite51†Hong et al. (2023) and ChatDev cite54†Qian et al. (2023) , construct system through handcrafted standard operating procedures (SOPs). Later works explore automating partial components of MAS: some generate agent roles using LLMs ( e.g., AgentVerse cite55†Chen et al. (2023) ), while others learn communication topologies with external modules (e.g., GPTSwarm cite56†Zhuge et al. (2024) ).\nL72: Recent studies pursue fully automated MAS construction, either by prompting LLMs to search for a single “optimal” system within a predefined design space (e.g., AgentSquare cite57†Shang et al. (2024) ), or by training a meta agent to generate systems for different queries (e.g., MAS-GPT cite58†Ye et al. (2025) ).\nL73: However, these methods lack dynamic adaptation at test time: one-size-fits-all systems cannot adjust their structure to different queries, while training-based approaches require substantial data and training before test. Moreover, most existing systems emphasize construction while overlooking the capacity for adaptive adjustment during execution, leading to brittle open-loop pipelines that cannot recover from single agent failures or unexpected environment feedback.\nL74: These limitations point to a key missing capability: Can we enable MAS to adapt dynamically at test time — both in how the system is constructed and how it executes — without any additional training?\nL75: To address this, we draw inspiration from biological adaptation: organisms utilize a conserved genetic toolkit encoding evolutionary experience to express diverse traits suited to varying environments (phenotypic plasticity), and leverage regulatory systems for real-time adjustment to maintain stability amidst internal and external perturbations (homeostatic regulation), as illustrated in Figure cite59†1 .\nL76: Inspired by this, we propose MASFly, a novel multi-agent framework that achieves on-the-fly adaptation without extra training, covering both adaptive system generation and execution.\nL77: Specifically, to navigate the combinatorially large design space of MAS, we mirror the logic of plasticity through a retrieval-augmented SOP instantiation mechanism for dynamic system generation. MASFly maintains an SOP repository that stores successful collaboration patterns across tasks, serving as the “genetic units” of the system.\nL78: Given a new query, a retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) process selects relevant patterns and adaptively expresses them, instantiating tailored agent roles and communication structures. Furthermore, to handle the non-stationary multi-agent environment—where agent actions reshape the context and external shifts occur unexpectedly—we incorporate a mechanism of homeostatic regulation via experience-guided process supervision.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFrom Fluent to Verifiable: Claim-Level Auditability forDeep Research Agents (https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13855v1)\nciteturn31894view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2602.13855v1\",\"lineno\":65}); Total lines: 329\nL58: The urgency is driven by a widening capability–credibility gap. Autonomous research agents can compress weeks-long cycles into hours, yet their most consequential steps—hypothesis formation, evidence selection, and causal attribution—often remain non-auditable after the fact (cite30†Hartung, 2025 ).\nL59: This is already visible in the ecosystem: AI-fabricated “junk science” is contaminating discovery layers such as Google Scholar, enabling downstream “evidence manipulation” where non-existent results are ingested, summarized, and eventually cited as record (cite31†Haider et al., 2024 ).\nL60: Even in well-intentioned deployments, failures can survive the “looks scientific” filter: an independent evaluation of Sakana’s The AI Scientist found that 42% of proposed experiments failed to execute due to coding errors, yet the system still produced manuscripts that mischaracterized established concepts as novel and relied on weak or flawed evidence chains (cite32†Beel and Kan, 2025 ).\nL61: Critically, the failure mode is shifting from wrong answers to wrong citations: analyses have surfaced hallucinated references in technical writing pipelines (cite33†, 2026 ) and even in the published record of elite venues, showing how attribution—central to reproducibility and credit assignment—can silently break under AI-mediated drafting (cite34†Goldman, 2026 ).\nL62: Meanwhile, the broader integrity ecosystem is under strain from industrial-scale fraud: Wiley’s Hindawi paper-mill investigations alone drove 11,300+ retractions by 2024 (cite35†Brundy, 2024 ; cite36†, 2024 ). The result is a looming reproducibility crisis of a new type: a deluge of technically fluent papers whose inferential lineage cannot be reconstructed, audited, or falsified because the reasoning and tool actions that generated them are opaque.\nL63: Auditability, therefore, is not merely a debugging aid; it is a governance primitive for scientific trust when discovery becomes autonomous.\nL64: ## 2. Background\nL65: Figure 1. The plan-execute-synthesize architecture of deep research agents. Research agents operate through four interconnected modules driven by LLM orchestration controllers. The planning module decomposes high-level objectives into task DAGs (44.2% of failures stem from specification errors (cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 )). The execution loop generates and runs code in sandboxed environments, parsing logs to debug autonomously (41-86.7% failure rates without persistent memory (cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 )).\nL66: The synthesis layer aggregates results into manuscripts. The reflexion loop provides automated review.\nL67: ### 2.1. The anatomy of autonomous discovery\nL68: Deep research agents share a common pipeline, even when implementations differ across The AI Scientist (cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ), Coscientist (cite38†Boiko et al., 2023 ), and ChemCrow (cite39†Bran et al., 2024 ). Most systems run a plan, execute, synthesize loop, where an LLM acts as the controller that assigns sub-tasks and routes tool calls (cite40†Sharma et al., 2025a ; cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 ). We divide the system into two phases, a ‘doing zone’ and a ‘thinking zone’ (see Figure cite41†1 ).\nL69: The doing zone produces evidence through planning and execution. The thinking zone turns evidence into a report through synthesis and review. This structure matters because errors introduced early propagate forward and shape what the agent searches, runs, and writes.\nL70: Planning step (Decomposition). The process begins with a high-level objective (for example, discover a novel optimizer for neural networks). A planning agent decomposes this into a directed acyclic graph (DAG) of sub-tasks (cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 ; cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ). For AI Scientist, this phase involves brainstorming ideas against a literature database to filter for novelty (cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ).\nL71: However, systematic evaluation reveals that this produces simplistic keyword searches rather than profound synthesis (cite42†Joeran Beel, 2025 ), leading to fundamental errors in hypothesis decomposition. Previous analysis (cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 ) of multi-agent planning shows that 44.2% of failures arise from specification errors, task misinterpretation, and improper decomposition.\nL72: These failures are consistent with broader patterns observed in agentic systems, in which errors propagate across layers from initial task specification to execution, creating cascading vulnerabilities that compromise downstream outputs (cite43†Adabara et al., 2025 ). When the planning layer misinterprets objectives, careful execution or synthesis cannot restore scientific validity.\nL73: Execution loop (Action step). The execution loop runs tools to carry out the plan. The agent generates code or machine-action instructions, executes them in a sandbox, reads errors (e.g., stderr), and debugs iteratively (cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ; cite38†Boiko et al., 2023 ; cite44†Szymanski et al., 2023 ). Tool-rich designs reduce some errors by adding feasibility checks; ChemCrow (cite39†Bran et al., 2024 ) integrates many expert tools to validate chemical constraints before acting.\nL74: However, execution still fails at high rates when the agent lacks persistent memory, because it repeatedly applies the same ineffective strategy across iterations (cite45†Besta et al., 2025 ; cite46†Rasmussen and Team, 2025 ). Earlier analysis (cite25†Cemri et al., 2025 ) across $\\sim$1,642 multi-agent system traces demonstrates systematic failure rates ranging from 41% to 86.7% depending on system architecture and task complexity.\nL75: These execution vulnerabilities align with autonomous-agent threat models;  shadow agent emergence arises when execution consistently diverges from the planned workflow without detection, and  tool squatting enables malicious behavior via compromised dependencies (cite43†Adabara et al., 2025 ). Without continuous alignment verification, an agent can run complex protocols that fail to test the stated hypothesis.\nL76: Synthesis step (Writing). The synthesis step aggregates tool outputs, logs, plots, and retrieved sources into a structured narrative (cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ; cite40†Sharma et al., 2025a ). This stage does more than summarizing. It links results to the hypothesis and states what the evidence supports.\nL77: When the planning or execution stage deviates from the objective, the synthesis stage often produce fluent text that obscures inadequate controls or weak evidential support, as it relies on the artifacts the system has accumulated.\nL78: The reflexion loop (Review). The reflexion loop adds a reviewer agent that critiques the draft and checks whether claims follow from the evidence (cite37†Lu et al., 2024 ; cite47†Du et al., 2023 ). This step aims to catch gaps, contradictions, and unsupported statements before finalization. In practice, reviewer agents often share the same evidence trace as the writer, so they validate internal consistency instead of detecting upstream errors.\n"
